{"attack": true, "case": "The target's central claim conflates 'error-eliminated' with 'having survived error-elimination.' The claim states the survivor is 'the most informative error-eliminated option available,' and the mechanism says we act on it because it is 'the only one not yet shown to be wrong.' But critical rationalism does not hold that testing removes errors from the surviving theory — the theory itself is unchanged by testing. What testing eliminates are *falsified competitors*, not errors *within* the surviving theory. The survivor remains a conjecture that may contain hidden errors; it has merely not yet been refuted. By characterizing the preferred theory as 'error-eliminated,' the target attributes to the survivor a property (freedom from eliminated errors) that the framework explicitly denies it can have. This undermines the wager's coherence: if the theory were genuinely error-eliminated, the wager would be unnecessary (we would have positive reason, not just strategic preference). If it is merely unrefuted, then 'error-eliminated' is a misnomer that overstates the epistemic achievement, and the 'most informative' designation becomes unjustified on the target's own non-inductive terms, since informativeness depends on the severity of tests — which the target's own countercondition admits may 'secretly rely on inductive assumptions about test validity.' The formulation thus either smuggles in an inductive conclusion (the theory is better because errors have been eliminated from it) or uses misleading language that the framework's own logic cannot support.", "premise": "Critical rationalism's error-elimination removes falsified *competitors*, not errors *within* the surviving theory, so calling the survivor 'error-eliminated' is a category error within the target's own stated framework.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "5ba7a0652869a36673791dc2f6fff77a7a20626eb0d4d0034c8ffe46ddd9754b", "quote": "making it the most informative error-eliminated option available."}, {"block": "5ba7a0652869a36673791dc2f6fff77a7a20626eb0d4d0034c8ffe46ddd9754b", "quote": "We act on the survivor because it is the only one not yet shown to be wrong in specific domains, maximizing our exposure to potential new errors (learning)."}], "counterexample": null, "successor_question": "Can the 'most informative error-eliminated option' language be salvaged without attributing to the surviving theory a freedom from error that critical rationalism explicitly denies — or must the claim be reformulated to say the survivor is 'least-refuted' rather than 'error-eliminated'?"}